WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.401
A logging helper has to take every list.

2
00:00:05.401 --> 00:00:06.868
A list of payment ids.

3
00:00:06.868 --> 00:00:08.669
A list of amounts in cents.

4
00:00:08.669 --> 00:00:11.336
It never needs to know the element type.

5
00:00:11.533 --> 00:00:17.385
Kotlin says that with a star projection: logAll takes a List&lt;*&gt;, and both lists fit.

6
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.880
Read List&lt;*&gt; as a list of one specific type, and you don't know which.

7
00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.081
Here star points at the list of ids.

8
00:00:24.081 --> 00:00:27.139
The compiler only knows it is a list of something.

9
00:00:27.139 --> 00:00:28.912
It could be the list of ints.

10
00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:30.179
Reading is safe.

11
00:00:30.179 --> 00:00:35.949
Whatever the list holds, each element comes out typed as Any?, which fits anything.

12
00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:37.996
Writing is not safe.

13
00:00:37.996 --> 00:00:41.651
star might be the list of ints, so no String may go in.

14
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.271
Kotlin doesn't even let null in, because the unknown type might be non-nullable.

15
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.895
Everything that doesn't take an element still works: size, isEmpty, removeAt and clear.

16
00:00:53.895 --> 00:00:58.232
So a function that empties any buffer can take a MutableList&lt;*&gt;.

17
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.825
Java writes the same idea as List&lt;?&gt;, the unbounded wildcard.

18
00:01:03.825 --> 00:01:06.672
Each element comes out as an Object.

19
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.455
A String can't go in on either side.

20
00:01:09.455 --> 00:01:17.715
Java is more relaxed about null: add(null) compiles on a List&lt;?&gt;, because every Java reference type allows null.

21
00:01:18.467 --> 00:01:21.521
What the star stands for depends on the declaration.

22
00:01:21.521 --> 00:01:24.517
Take Source, declared out T, with the bound Number.

23
00:01:24.733 --> 00:01:28.857
A Source&lt;*&gt; might be a Source&lt;Int&gt;, or a Source&lt;BigDecimal&gt;.

24
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.790
Whichever it is, next returns some Number.

25
00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:35.776
So reads keep the bound: Source&lt;*&gt; means Source&lt;out Number&gt;.

26
00:01:36.533 --> 00:01:38.614
Now take Sink, declared in T.

27
00:01:38.614 --> 00:01:42.989
A Sink&lt;*&gt; might be a Sink&lt;String&gt;, or a Sink&lt;CardAuthorized&gt;.

28
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.227
A String fits only the first.

29
00:01:45.227 --> 00:01:48.163
A card authorization fits only the second.

30
00:01:48.333 --> 00:01:53.085
No value is accepted by every one of them, so the compiler allows none.

31
00:01:53.085 --> 00:01:56.497
Nothing is Kotlin's name for a type with no values.

32
00:01:56.497 --> 00:01:58.572
Sink&lt;*&gt; means Sink&lt;in Nothing&gt;.

33
00:01:59.333 --> 00:02:04.055
So a star is shorthand for the safest projection the declaration allows.

34
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.649
An invariant Box gets both rules.

35
00:02:06.649 --> 00:02:11.324
A Box&lt;*&gt; reads as Box&lt;out Any?&gt;, and writes as Box&lt;in Nothing&gt;.

36
00:02:11.324 --> 00:02:16.593
Assign to its item, and the compiler names the type it wanted: Nothing.

37
00:02:17.467 --> 00:02:20.601
Java's question mark follows the same rules.

38
00:02:20.601 --> 00:02:24.020
The bound survives it, so peek returns a Number.

39
00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:26.370
A String can't go into a Sink&lt;?&gt;.

40
00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:29.291
Only null gets through, and only in Java.
